"""VO beats for How to Buy a Cessna 182 Skylane (Ep5, N23383, 1998 182S).
Story cut (Ep3 v2 / Ep4 shape): title -> choose -> ladder -> shortlist -> the one we ran -> report sections as questions -> offer -> keep watching -> verdict.
Plain-language standard (Alex 2026-10-09): every number gets the pattern, a "because" that names a person, and the so-what.
Numbers as words, no ellipses, sentences of about 20 words or fewer."""
BEATS = [
("b01", "title", "This is How to Buy a Cessna one eighty-two Skylane. One real airplane for sale right now, one real NextPlane report, and the questions a careful buyer asks."),
("b02", "problem", "Step one. Choose which one. The Skylane is the bigger, stronger sibling of the Cessna one seventy-two. It seats four, and Cessna has built it, on and off, since nineteen fifty-six. This week, NextPlane is tracking one hundred fifty Skylanes for sale in the U S. One hundred twenty-three show a price."),
("b03", "ladder", "The first question is which era, because the era sets the price. We use the typical asking price: half ask more, half ask less. Skylanes from the fifties and sixties ask about one hundred forty-five thousand dollars. The mid-seventies models, about one hundred eighty thousand. The late seventies to mid-eighties, about two hundred twenty-seven thousand. Then Cessna stopped building small airplanes for about ten years, largely because of lawsuit costs. The restart model, the one eighty-two S, asks about three hundred twenty thousand. Two thousands models, about three hundred sixty thousand. The newest, about six hundred fifty thousand."),
("b04", "shortlist", "Now narrow it down. Say you want a Skylane built after the restart, but not a new one. That is the one eighty-two S, built from nineteen ninety-seven to two thousand. Seventeen are for sale, and sixteen show a price. They run from about two hundred twenty-five thousand to about four hundred ninety-five thousand dollars. Same model, more than double the money. So we ran one of the cheapest, to see why it is cheap."),
("b05", "pick", "This one. A nineteen ninety-eight one eighty-two S, listed by a private seller in North Carolina. Asking two hundred seventy-nine thousand dollars. That is forty thousand under NextPlane's estimate. A bargain? Here is what stopped us. The report puts its running cost at two dollars and sixty-seven cents a mile. The average one eighty-two S for sale runs one dollar fifty-one."),
("b06", "header", "Step two. Evaluate the one. Open the NextPlane Aircraft Report. The top section is the summary, with warnings and good news in one place. No record in the federal accident database, the N T S B. No loan recorded against it in the last year of F A A filings. The same company has owned it for three and a half years. And one warning: the engine is near its overhaul. But first, is the price fair?"),
("b07", "value", "This section is the valuation, NextPlane's estimate of what a similar airplane is worth today, built from comparable listings. The estimate is three hundred nineteen thousand dollars. The middle half of similar airplanes ask about three hundred to three hundred fifty-five thousand. This one asks forty thousand less, about twelve percent under. A hopeful buyer thinks: bargain. A careful buyer asks: why?"),
("b08", "engine", "The answer is the engine. This section, true cost per mile, adds up fuel, upkeep, and saving for the next engine overhaul, a full rebuild. The engine maker recommends one every two thousand hours. The listing says this engine has one thousand six hundred ninety-nine hours since new, never overhauled. So only about three hundred hours remain. The average one eighty-two S for sale has about twelve hundred left. The report prices an overhaul at about sixty-five thousand dollars. So the missing engine life is worth about twenty-nine thousand."),
("b09", "adjust", "Take the estimate, three hundred nineteen thousand. Take off the engine, about twenty-nine thousand. That leaves about two hundred ninety thousand dollars. NextPlane's price model, which scores every listing each week, lands in the same place. And five other one eighty-two S airplanes with engines past fourteen hundred hours asked a typical two hundred eighty-two thousand. So this is not a bargain. It is priced about right, for an engine that is nearly due."),
("b10", "overhaul", "Another way to see it. Buy this one and overhaul the engine, and you are in for about three hundred forty-four thousand dollars. A nineteen ninety-nine with a freshly overhauled engine asks three hundred thirty-nine thousand, nine hundred. Nearly the same money. The real difference is who picks the shop, and when."),
("b11", "pricehist", "This section is the price history, every asking-price change NextPlane recorded. Has the seller moved? Five times in one month. Listed on September fifth at three hundred twenty-nine thousand dollars. Then ten thousand off, five times, most recently on October sixth. That is fifty thousand in all, about fifteen percent. Among Skylanes listed this spring and summer, about four in ten cut their price. The typical total cut was under seven percent. So this seller has cut more than twice the typical amount, in about a month. We do not know why. Ask."),
("b12", "owner", "Who owns it? This section comes from the F A A registry: the registered owner, how long they have held it, and any recorded loans. The owner is a North Carolina company, registered since April twenty twenty-three. But the listing comes from a private person. That often happens when a business owns the airplane. Confirm the seller can sign for the company. Run a title search, which checks for claims against the airplane. And pay through escrow, a neutral party that holds the money."),
("b13", "flight", "Is it flying? This section uses A D S B, the position signal most airplanes broadcast, to track real flights. Over two years, we see about forty-four hours. Most came in one winter, from November twenty twenty-four to March twenty twenty-five. Since then, only about eleven hours. Mechanics often say an engine that sits ages faster, because moisture gets inside. That is a common shop view, not something we measured. So ask for an oil analysis, and a camera look inside the cylinders."),
("b14", "ads", "What should the logbooks show? This section lists mandatory safety fixes, called airworthiness directives, that the F A A issued for this model. The report matches eleven to this airplane. Seven only apply if certain parts are installed. Four apply to every one eighty-two S: the oil pressure switch, the seat locks, and the exhaust mufflers. Have your mechanic confirm each is signed off. The listing says the annual inspection was done September first. Check that entry too."),
("b15", "sdr", "Has it ever been hurt? The listing reports no damage. NextPlane finds no federal accident record. The F A A's service problem reports show one entry, from the year two thousand. An autopilot part was found faulty during a factory-recommended check. Old, minor, and worth one question."),
("b16", "opcost", "What will it cost to keep? This section estimates a year of ownership. At one hundred twenty hours a year, about twenty-seven thousand seven hundred dollars, or two hundred thirty-one an hour. Fuel is the biggest line. On this airplane, add the overhaul, about sixty-five thousand dollars, within about two and a half years at that pace."),
("b17", "seller", "And who am I dealing with? A private seller, not a dealer. Private sellers are often more flexible on price. But there is no dealer warranty to lean on. So budget for an independent pre-buy inspection, at a shop you choose."),
("b18", "offer", "Step three. Price the offer. Start with the engine-adjusted value, about two hundred ninety thousand dollars. Then look at the closest match for sale. A nineteen ninety-nine with its original engine at eighteen hundred twenty-two hours asks two hundred forty thousand. It has been listed for more than a hundred days. This seller has been cutting ten thousand at a time. And NextPlane's model gives about a three in four chance of another cut within ninety days. So a reasonable opening offer is around two hundred sixty-five thousand dollars, about five percent under the ask. Make it subject to the pre-buy, the oil analysis, and the cylinder check. If the engine fails those, the overhaul is now, not later. Then ask for the full overhaul cost off, or walk away."),
("b19", "watch", "Step four. Keep watching. In the last two weeks, fourteen Skylanes were newly listed. Sellers made twenty-two price cuts, and only two increases. Right now, sellers are the ones adjusting."),
("b20", "verdict", "So who is this airplane right for? A buyer who plans to overhaul soon, and wants to choose the shop. You get a low-time airframe, about seventeen hundred hours, where the average Skylane for sale has about thirty-six hundred. It is not right for you if you want three hundred hours of flying before spending on the engine. Priced about right for its engine, with a clean history. Check three things: the engine's condition, who signs for the company, and that September annual."),
("b21", "end", "Before you call the broker, check the data at NextPlane dot U S."),
]
if __name__ == "__main__":
    import re
    n = sum(len(t.split()) for _, _, t in BEATS); print("words", n)
    assert not any("..." in t for _, _, t in BEATS)
    for b, _, t in BEATS:
        for s in re.split(r"(?<=[.?!])\s+", t):
            if len(s.split()) > 22: print("LONG", b, len(s.split()), s)
